deviations:
F2(a): instead, I made the sound tightenings available:
- one chain of in-place evaluations per value for the stack, the cycle check and the dynamic-scope walk;
- lower and upper bounds that cannot fail together count once;
- integer literals never fail an integer type (new internal kind kindInteger);
- propertyNames wrappers are charged only for names that can fail.

Name-hash charges are conservative:
- every evaluation with member keywords counts one hash per name, though the library hashes only when a properties map has more than 8 entries;
- each unevaluated copy counts 5 hashes;
- uniqueItems comparisons of objects are charged per name for every possible pair;
- the new dimObjects count bounds const and enum object comparisons, and each enclosing value's count is added to its descendants.
The long-name benchmark cases therefore run at 0.03 to 0.26 ns per unit.

lenses.summary:
Spec-conformance review of WP-51, round 4. I reviewed every file in internal/filter/validation/jsonschema/ against wps.json, architecture 4.4, R-63 and R-77 (the user's decisions), spec 07 groups J and K, section 9 items 21 to 24, and the configuration model's "Body buffering and limits" rule.

Verdict: fix. One blocker remains.

**Checks.** All scoped checks pass:
- CGO_ENABLED=0 go test -count=1 -shuffle=on (44.6 s) and go test -race -count=1 -shuffle=on (52.3 s);
- go vet, with and without -tags integration;
- bin/golangci-lint run ./internal/filter/validation/jsonschema/... (0 issues);
- go run ./internal/tool/repocheck;
- the integration JSON-Schema-Test-Suite (46 files, 384 groups, 24 refused, 1,248 cases).

No stray files are left in the repository.

**What conforms:**
- **Runtime statuses and codes:**
  - 400 RZ-RT-009 with the exact fixed details of 07 reqs 78 to 81 and R-77's "Request body has a number out of range";
  - 413 through filter.ErrTooLarge for the decoded limit, the 8x working set, the work limit, the 12x reservation cap and the nesting limit;
  - 503 through filter.ErrBudget;
  - cannot decide only for internal or library errors (cancellation stays the known minor contract request).
- **Compile refusals:** RZ-CFG-005, from both Factory.Build and Check, at spec.config.schema paths.
- **Round-3 fixes:**
  - F3: a 12x cap applies before Reserve, and the location pass also checks it.
  - F4: the contains matched-list charge is in place.
  - F5: the propertyNames wrapper rule is min(W, full).
  - F6: there are regression tests for every surviving mutant.
  - The minors were addressed.
- **R-63/R-77 fit:** typical records fit. Probed at the 10 MiB default through Factory.Build and Handle, the largest valid body accepted was:
  - Petstore arrays (additionalProperties false, enum, nested refs): 86%;
  - user arrays (uniqueItems roles) and customer arrays: 100%;
  - metrics with an additionalProperties map: 97%;
  - allOf with unevaluatedProperties false: 100%;
  - nullable oneOf/anyOf refs: 78%;
  - orders with 5 line items: 72%;
  - a 3-way discriminated union: 70%.

**Blocker: the round-3 F1 fix is incomplete.** The instruction caps ignore character-class ranges multiplied by repetition.
- Go's one-pass compiler copies each instruction's class into the compiled Schema. A 4.6 KB schema of 98 "^\\pL{990}" patterns builds in 1.5 to 3 s, and the built Filter keeps 864 MB.
- The package's own classReps sorts instructions times ranges before any budget applies. A 60 KB "[20,000 CJK chars]{990}" pattern takes 3 to 6 s; four such patterns in 240 KB take 18 s, allocate 5 GB and keep 899 MB.
- This breaks F6's 1 s, the 0.5 s / 9 MB compile figure R-77 records, and the doc.go claims.

**Minors:**
- The maxFoldRunes refusal is outside R-77's decided list.
- A few comments are still broken across lines.

Probes: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-ruralz/0aabfe6e-34a6-51d2-9b9b-23886b703760/scratchpad/r4spec/p/ (zz_r4class_test.go, zz_r4esc*_test.go, zz_r4build_test.go, zz_r4fit*_test.go, overlay.json).

Round-5 review of WP-51, lens: resource bounds and hostile inputs. Scoped checks pass on the current working tree: `CGO_ENABLED=0 go test -count=1 -shuffle=on ./internal/filter/validation/jsonschema/...` (48.5 s) and `go vet`. The targeted bound tests (TestWorkingSetCoversPeak, TestHandleBoundsUnevaluatedMemory, TestHandleBoundsMatchedItems, TestHandleWorkBound, TestHandleReservationLimit, TestHandleMeasuredFit, TestHandleAcceptsLargeBodies, TestStackWithinCharge) also pass.

I read filter.go, schema.go, validate.go, working.go, work.go, states.go and doc.go against the jsonschema/v6 v6.0.3 validator.go and util.go. The model covers the earlier findings as fixed:
- unevaluated-name and index copies along in-place chains (counted in dimUneval, summed on the path);
- contains matched lists;
- propertyNames wrappers;
- decision-pass array loops that do not stop early;
- uniqueItems hash classes, including integers colliding with their negation and decimal numerator/denominator splits;
- the stack charge per chain;
- the 12x reservation cap.

I found no case where the reservation is smaller than the memory the library keeps live.

One major gap remains: the work model has no per-byte term for member names. The library hashes each name on every evaluation at its object, through the properties lookup, the unevaluated copies, merges and deletes, and the uniqueItems object comparisons. A measured lookup into a map of more than 8 entries costs about 0.5 ms for a 10 MiB key. At MaxEvaluations that comes to seconds of uncharged, uncancellable CPU, against milliseconds charged.

Limits of this review: that figure is computed from the per-lookup measurement, not measured end to end through Handle. The per-shape peak-heap, CPU and reservation measurements the lens called for (unevaluated chains, wide enums, patternProperties, uniqueItems, deep nesting, many-error bodies) were not done. My first measurement attempt was cut off by a safety stop, and I did not resume hostile-input probing. Beyond the package's own tests passing, those shapes were checked only by reading the code.

No repository file was changed. My only scratch file is the standalone hash-timing program at /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-ruralz/0aabfe6e-34a6-51d2-9b9b-23886b703760/scratchpad/r5/hash/main.go.

confirmed.fix:
1. For any pattern that can compile one-pass, add the one-pass matcher to the walk's measure. A pattern can compile one-pass when its program starts anchored at begin-text (^ or \A, EmptyBeginText) and its instruction estimate is under 1,000, Go's makeOnePass limit. Compute each instruction's first-rune set size bottom-up on the syntax.Prog that syntax.Compile builds from the already bounded tree:
   - a rune instruction: its ranges;
   - an Alt: the sum of both branches;
   - a capture, nop or empty-width instruction: its successor's.
   Then charge, at rangesPerInstruction ranges per instruction:
   - each rune instruction: about one more instruction for its copy and Next table, plus its ranges;
   - each Alt: its first-rune set size, which for an alternation of k branches comes to about k(k+1)/2 times the branches' first-rune ranges.
   Alternatively, refuse such patterns once their tables pass a stated size.
2. Add these cases to TestPatternProgramMemory and to BenchmarkCompileAnalysisWorstCase, and keep the 10 MiB assertion (the documented bound):
   - an anchored alternation of disjoint classes (the 23 x 330-branch schema);
   - the 131-alternation schema alt131.json;
   - 131 x ^[a-bd-eg-hj-k]{990}N$;
   - the quest form ^\pL{1,115}$.
3. Correct doc.go's kept and held figures (:83-88, :102-108) to the bound the fixed measure gives, and correct the compile contract request that feeds R-77's 9 MB figure.

confirmed.issue:
The work model charges each member a fixed cost per evaluation, whatever the length of its name. The library hashes the whole name on several per-evaluation paths:
- the properties lookup;
- each unevaluated-names copy;
- each merge and delete;
- the map lookups when uniqueItems compares object items.
These costs grow with name bytes times evaluations, and no term charges them. Go's small-map path (8 entries or fewer) compares lengths and skips the hash; above that size, every lookup hashes the full key.

Measured cost of the hash alone, timing a lookup of a key absent from a 9-entry map[string]bool (standalone go run, scratchpad r5/hash/main.go, Go 1.27.1, 4 vCPUs):
- 1 KiB key: 34 ns;
- 64 KiB: 1.45 us;
- 1 MiB: 27 us;
- 10 MiB: 500 us per lookup (about 21 to 45 GB/s).
With a 1-entry map, the 10 MiB lookup took 41 ns. The gap therefore needs a properties map of more than 8 names, which is common, or an unevaluated copy of an object with more than 8 members.

Estimate (computed from the per-lookup cost, not measured end to end through Factory.Build and Handle): at the 10 MiB default, member names can total about 10 MiB, and one object value may take up to MaxEvaluations (4,096) evaluations. That gives about 4,096 x 0.5 ms, or about 2 s, of uncharged, uncancellable hashing for the properties lookups alone. The model charges about 4,096 x (384 + 48) units, roughly 2 ms. Along an unevaluatedProperties chain, the insert, merge lookup and delete roughly triple the cost. The unevaluated-copy memory stays small, so the working-set limit does not stop it.

The documented per-term calibration ('each term measured and rounded up') and the 'at most about 2.7 s' figure therefore do not hold for bodies with long member names. No benchmark covers per-byte work on member names that no pattern matches.

remainingMinor.issue:
Several common dense valid shapes fit well below the documented 'typical records 70 to 84%', but the fit list does not mention them. Measured through Factory.Build and Handle at the 10 MiB default, largest valid body that passes:
- (spec) an array of decimals under {"type":"number","minimum":-1000,"maximum":1000}: 60.4%, working-set limit (decision charge 13.2x the body), against 80.7% with type number alone;
- (spec) [timestamp, value] pairs under prefixItems integer/number: 45.8%, working-set limit;
- (bounds) arrays of [lon, lat] pairs under {"type":"array","minItems":2,"maxItems":2,"items":{"type":"number","minimum":-180,"maximum":180}}: 35.1% (3,683,471 B), working-set limit (decide 83.8 MB, about 416 bytes per 18-byte pair, peak heap 30.7 MB);
- (bounds) time series {"points":[{"t":int,"v":number},...]} with required and additionalProperties false: 48.1% (5,040,276 B), reservation limit;
- (spec) uniqueItems array of integer ids: 33.1%, and of short string tags: 62.1%, both working-set limit (duplicates map charged 192 bytes per item).
The charges are sound (the library's arrays never stop early in boolean mode) and within the accepted R-77 envelope; only the documentation is incomplete, so operators cannot see when to raise maxRequestBodyBytes. Typical records otherwise fit as R-77 states (OpenAPI-style nullable users 99.9%, 64-byte-key maps 99%, allOf inheritance 81%, JSON:API 67%). Probes: zz_r6spec_test.go TestR6MapFit and TestR6MoreFit (bisected to 1%).
